{
  "dom_id": "step-q250654",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-2(x+1)(x-3).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-2<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=3,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-1+3}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2(x+1)(x-3) \\\\  & =-2(1+1)(1-3) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,8),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,8],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228566/assets/a9c15f73f41c2c58fdbc05eceb3031edfb6f04f12db9fa54450f6445b196d1df.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
